WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.173
This is a technical deep dive
from the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:04.173 --> 00:00:07.249
Workbook's Research section,
and it's still under review.

4
00:00:07.249 --> 00:00:13.184
It's about Infoblox DDI, meaning
DNS, DHCP and IP address management,

5
00:00:13.184 --> 00:00:19.225
on Infoblox's NIOS Grid and its
cloud-managed service, Universal DDI.

6
00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:23.135
It's for the architects and engineers
deciding whether to build an

7
00:00:23.135 --> 00:00:27.188
agency's name and address layer on
Infoblox, and how to run it if they do.

8
00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:31.915
After a short orientation, Part 1 covers
the platform and the case for it,

9
00:00:31.915 --> 00:00:35.600
including FedRAMP scope, the
competition and the risks.

10
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.250
Part 2 covers how a central team hands
DDI to divisions without losing control.

11
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:46.110
Every fact comes from a primary source
opened on October twenty-second,

12
00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.430
2026, and marketing is
called a vendor claim.

13
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.567
Here's why this matters.

14
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:58.992
Almost every connection an agency
makes starts with a DNS lookup,

15
00:00:58.992 --> 00:01:04.318
and in a hybrid, multi-cloud estate that
answer decides far more than an address.

16
00:01:04.318 --> 00:01:07.508
DHCP and address management
sit right beside it,

17
00:01:07.508 --> 00:01:11.177
because lease history is how
an address maps to a device,

18
00:01:11.177 --> 00:01:15.643
and the address plan is the record
behind the inventory directives.

19
00:01:15.643 --> 00:01:19.117
So this deck treats all
three as one control plane.

20
00:01:19.117 --> 00:01:24.006
Book 08 of the workbook describes that
plane: on-premises servers that answer,

21
00:01:24.006 --> 00:01:27.214
a management plane that
holds the system of record,

22
00:01:27.214 --> 00:01:30.880
and local resolvers that keep
working if it's unreachable.

23
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.055
The argument of the session is simple.

24
00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:35.723
The rules need one governed DDI plane,

25
00:01:35.723 --> 00:01:41.600
and Infoblox is one of the strongest ways
to build it, with real risks to manage.

26
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.125
Here's what the rules actually
require, stated as capabilities.

27
00:01:49.125 --> 00:01:55.675
Sign agency zones and validate answers,
with the 2008 DNSSEC memo still in force.

28
00:01:55.675 --> 00:01:59.497
Use encrypted DNS wherever
it's technically supported,

29
00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:02.737
and forward to CISA's
Protective DNS upstream.

30
00:02:02.737 --> 00:02:08.197
Logging is subtle: the May 2026 memo that
replaced the old logging memo doesn't

31
00:02:08.197 --> 00:02:13.741
mention DNS at all, so DNS and DHCP logging
is carried forward by CISA's Logging

32
00:02:13.741 --> 00:02:19.200
Reference Architecture, with six months
searchable and twelve retrievable.

33
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.213
And the inventory directives
want regular discovery of agency

34
00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:27.225
address space and attestation of
public addresses and domains.

35
00:02:27.225 --> 00:02:28.913
Now the honest starting point.

36
00:02:28.913 --> 00:02:31.707
No rule names Infoblox or any DDI vendor,

37
00:02:31.707 --> 00:02:35.710
so choosing a platform is
an architecture decision.

38
00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.360
Module 1 is the platform.

39
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.890
The names have changed and a lot
of older material is now wrong,

40
00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:50.952
so we'll get an accurate picture: the
Grid, the cloud side and its limits,

41
00:02:50.952 --> 00:02:55.210
DNS, DHCP and IPAM, security,
automation and logging.

42
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.662
The NIOS Grid is a group of appliances
sharing one distributed database,

43
00:03:03.662 --> 00:03:08.835
run from a single point, the Grid Master,
which every administrator goes through.

44
00:03:08.835 --> 00:03:13.104
Members can be physical hardware or
virtual appliances, singly or in

45
00:03:13.104 --> 00:03:18.122
high-availability pairs, and they talk to
the Grid Master over encrypted tunnels.

46
00:03:18.122 --> 00:03:20.819
A Grid Master Candidate holds a full copy,

47
00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:25.022
but promoting it is a manual job
you can't do from the cloud portal.

48
00:03:25.022 --> 00:03:29.785
The current hardware is Trinzic X6,
and the virtual appliances run on

49
00:03:29.785 --> 00:03:34.798
VMware and every major cloud, with
Azure Government explicitly covered.

50
00:03:34.798 --> 00:03:39.498
On versions, NIOS 9.1.0
went GA in March 2026,

51
00:03:39.498 --> 00:03:44.198
and 9.0.6 started a
long-term-support train.

52
00:03:44.198 --> 00:03:46.610
Next, the cloud-managed side.

53
00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:56.374
Universal DDI is the cloud-managed side,
renamed from BloxOne DDI in September 2024,

54
00:03:56.374 --> 00:03:59.948
and run from what's now
called the Infoblox Portal.

55
00:03:59.948 --> 00:04:05.529
Its servers run either on your infrastructure
or as a service from Infoblox's points

56
00:04:05.529 --> 00:04:09.197
of presence, and its management
plane reaches out to the

57
00:04:09.197 --> 00:04:12.785
major cloud DNS services and
to Microsoft DNS and DHCP.

58
00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.995
Infoblox markets it as a single
control plane; that's a vendor claim,

59
00:04:16.995 --> 00:04:20.210
and Infoblox's own pages
show why you should test it.

60
00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:25.498
The Portal can't promote a Grid Master
Candidate or manage DHCP failover, the

61
00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:31.122
as-a-service option has no IPv6, and the
cloud DNS sync handles only simple records.

62
00:04:31.122 --> 00:04:37.070
So "one console" is overstated: in practice
you'll run both consoles side by side.

63
00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:45.208
DNS on NIOS is strong, with
a catch for each feature.

64
00:04:45.208 --> 00:04:48.670
DNS views give different
answers to different clients,

65
00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:52.885
and that's how you'd point internal
traffic at CISA's Protective DNS.

66
00:04:52.885 --> 00:04:57.777
DNSSEC signing is central, done only by
the Grid Master as a hidden primary, with

67
00:04:57.777 --> 00:05:02.970
keys there or on an external hardware
security module, and automatic key rollovers.

68
00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:07.500
DNS Firewall blocks bad names
with response policy zones,

69
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:13.445
the Grid can manage Windows DNS and DHCP
servers without an agent, and from 9.1,

70
00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:17.314
encrypted DNS is served,
but only on X6 appliances,

71
00:05:17.314 --> 00:05:20.145
free there and an extra license on X5.

72
00:05:20.145 --> 00:05:25.575
And one setting you must change: the
default signing algorithm is RSA with SHA-1,

73
00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:28.583
so pick something
stronger before you sign.

74
00:05:28.583 --> 00:05:31.660
Next, DHCP and IPAM.

75
00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:36.935
DHCP first.

76
00:05:36.935 --> 00:05:39.693
Failover peers talk to
each other directly;

77
00:05:39.693 --> 00:05:43.523
the Grid Master isn't involved,
whatever older material says.

78
00:05:43.523 --> 00:05:48.347
But failover is IPv4 only, which
matters under the IPv6 mandate,

79
00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:50.759
though Windows has the same gap.

80
00:05:50.759 --> 00:05:55.723
Lease history is archived, which is the
address-to-asset record NIST asks for.

81
00:05:55.723 --> 00:05:59.537
On the IPAM side, a network view
is a single routing domain,

82
00:05:59.537 --> 00:06:04.830
and discovery tools read both the network
and the clouds, though the deeper device

83
00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:09.811
discovery needs its own license and
dedicated appliances, so budget for that.

84
00:06:09.811 --> 00:06:11.856
Two corrections to common claims:

85
00:06:11.856 --> 00:06:15.510
separate network views permit
overlap, they don't prevent it.

86
00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:19.848
And the AWS address-management
integration excludes GovCloud.

87
00:06:19.848 --> 00:06:22.100
Now, the security layer.

88
00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:30.175
Infoblox Threat Defense is the
DNS-layer security product.

89
00:06:30.175 --> 00:06:33.388
It blocks bad names, shares
threat intelligence,

90
00:06:33.388 --> 00:06:38.850
and detects things like DNS tunneling,
data exfiltration and command-and-control.

91
00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:44.607
It runs from the cloud or on premises, and
one detection mode streams DNS metadata,

92
00:06:44.607 --> 00:06:47.528
including client addresses, to the cloud,

93
00:06:47.528 --> 00:06:51.137
so treat that as a boundary
decision, not a checkbox.

94
00:06:51.137 --> 00:06:55.587
Licensing comes in four tiers, with
the cloud pieces in the upper two.

95
00:06:55.587 --> 00:07:00.594
There's one strong independent signal:
Google Cloud's own documentation

96
00:07:00.594 --> 00:07:05.100
describes its DNS Armor threat
detection as powered by Infoblox.

97
00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.438
Then there are the marketing figures about
how many threats it stops and how early.

98
00:07:09.438 --> 00:07:11.752
Nobody independent has verified them,

99
00:07:11.752 --> 00:07:14.870
so keep them out of
anything an assessor reads.

100
00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:23.058
Automation and logging decide whether
the platform can be governed at scale.

101
00:07:23.058 --> 00:07:27.495
On automation, Infoblox is
ahead of the other DDI vendors.

102
00:07:27.495 --> 00:07:30.934
NIOS has a REST API with
modern sign-in from 9.1,

103
00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:37.068
and the Terraform provider moved to a 3.0
beta on October thirteenth that covers

104
00:07:37.068 --> 00:07:43.295
both NIOS and Universal DDI but isn't
backward compatible, so plan that migration.

105
00:07:43.295 --> 00:07:46.320
Ansible and ServiceNow
integrations exist too.

106
00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.008
Logging has sharper edges.

107
00:07:48.008 --> 00:07:53.171
Full query logging adds load, the dnstap
stream drops logs if its receiver is down,

108
00:07:53.171 --> 00:07:56.330
and the built-in reporting
appliances lock after

109
00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:59.258
repeated overruns of
their daily ingest caps.

110
00:07:59.258 --> 00:08:04.196
So for federal retention, send logs to
the SIEM and size reporting deliberately.

111
00:08:04.196 --> 00:08:06.170
That completes the platform.

112
00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.583
Module 2 is the case for Infoblox.

113
00:08:12.583 --> 00:08:16.137
We'll map each federal requirement
to an Infoblox feature,

114
00:08:16.137 --> 00:08:20.506
read exactly what the FedRAMP listing
covers, compare the competition,

115
00:08:20.506 --> 00:08:25.750
and lay out the risks, because a recommendation
that hides its risks isn't usable.

116
00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.528
This is the core of the
argument: each requirement,

117
00:08:32.528 --> 00:08:35.925
the Infoblox feature that
delivers it, and what to watch.

118
00:08:35.925 --> 00:08:41.019
Signing and resilient DNS map to the
Grid Master and Grid replication,

119
00:08:41.019 --> 00:08:45.937
but candidate promotion is manual,
so it belongs in a tested runbook.

120
00:08:45.937 --> 00:08:49.788
Encrypted DNS works on X6 hardware only.

121
00:08:49.788 --> 00:08:54.231
Protective DNS forwarding works
per view, but no Infoblox document

122
00:08:54.231 --> 00:08:58.437
was found on encrypted forwarding
to CISA, so confirm it in a lab.

123
00:08:58.437 --> 00:09:03.151
Logging and inventory are well covered,
with the caps and hardware we just saw.

124
00:09:03.151 --> 00:09:06.962
IPv6 is there, but without DHCP failover.

125
00:09:06.962 --> 00:09:12.176
And validated cryptography is the real
gap, with no active certificate today.

126
00:09:12.176 --> 00:09:17.287
Still, seven of eight requirements
land on one platform and one database.

127
00:09:17.287 --> 00:09:19.770
That consolidation is the case.

128
00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.578
FedRAMP is the most misquoted
part of any Infoblox proposal,

129
00:09:27.578 --> 00:09:29.120
so let's read the record.

130
00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.992
The Marketplace lists Infoblox Government
Cloud as FedRAMP Certified at Moderate,

131
00:09:34.992 --> 00:09:40.445
as SaaS in AWS GovCloud, with one agency
authorization and a reuse count of zero.

132
00:09:40.445 --> 00:09:43.913
It covers Universal DDI
and some security services,

133
00:09:43.913 --> 00:09:48.832
but not everything; ask Infoblox for
the authorization boundary diagram.

134
00:09:48.832 --> 00:09:53.161
And the biggest point: NIOS appliances
and virtual appliances on agency

135
00:09:53.161 --> 00:09:57.921
infrastructure aren't a cloud service,
so no FedRAMP authorization covers them.

136
00:09:57.921 --> 00:10:00.802
They sit inside the agency's own boundary,

137
00:10:00.802 --> 00:10:03.683
and that's the path for
High or DoD systems.

138
00:10:03.683 --> 00:10:07.482
With reuse at zero, expect
to do your own ATO work.

139
00:10:07.482 --> 00:10:12.680
Ask, too, for a list of features the
FedRAMP instance has versus commercial.

140
00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:19.559
A narrower question: if
an agency buys Trinzic,

141
00:10:19.559 --> 00:10:24.417
the physical appliances, how well does
the hardware fit Book 08's design?

142
00:10:24.417 --> 00:10:29.141
The Grid gives one shared database with
an HA Grid Master and a candidate,

143
00:10:29.141 --> 00:10:33.551
though promotion is manual and
Infoblox publishes no scale limits,

144
00:10:33.551 --> 00:10:35.204
so size it with the vendor.

145
00:10:35.204 --> 00:10:39.079
Domain controllers can send
Kerberos-signed updates to it,

146
00:10:39.079 --> 00:10:41.767
signing is central, and IPAM is built in.

147
00:10:41.767 --> 00:10:46.787
The watch points are the ones we've
seen: change the signing default, X6 for

148
00:10:46.787 --> 00:10:52.392
free encrypted DNS, dedicated appliances
for deep discovery, and reporting caps.

149
00:10:52.392 --> 00:10:57.156
Trinzic sits inside the agency's
boundary, outside the FedRAMP listing.

150
00:10:57.156 --> 00:11:01.650
And its FIPS 140-2 certificate
went historical in September,

151
00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.717
with newer modules pending.

152
00:11:03.717 --> 00:11:05.570
Next, the clouds.

153
00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:14.382
Can Trinzic run an agency's DDI alone
once workloads move to the clouds?

154
00:11:14.382 --> 00:11:16.583
On its own, it can see into them,

155
00:11:16.583 --> 00:11:22.270
discovering networks and importing zones
one way, but it can't own cloud DNS.

156
00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.638
There are three ways to
extend it, and they combine:

157
00:11:25.638 --> 00:11:28.924
virtual appliances in each
cloud, Universal DDI,

158
00:11:28.924 --> 00:11:33.031
which is the only Infoblox path
that writes back to cloud DNS,

159
00:11:33.031 --> 00:11:37.795
or native cloud resolvers wired to
the Grid with forwarding both ways.

160
00:11:37.795 --> 00:11:40.797
The design guidance is
this: mostly on premises,

161
00:11:40.797 --> 00:11:45.262
use native resolvers with forwarding;
hybrid, add virtual appliances;

162
00:11:45.262 --> 00:11:47.957
multi-cloud at Moderate,
add Universal DDI;

163
00:11:47.957 --> 00:11:50.805
in GovCloud or Azure
Government at Moderate,

164
00:11:50.805 --> 00:11:55.347
confirm the government edition
supports those accounts first; and for

165
00:11:55.347 --> 00:12:00.120
FedRAMP High or the higher Defense
levels, don't depend on the Moderate SaaS.

166
00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:03.090
The Trinzic explainer
has the full reasoning.

167
00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.228
Now the assurance evidence,
checked on October twenty-second.

168
00:12:10.228 --> 00:12:15.720
There's no C I S Benchmark for NIOS, so
the hardening baseline is the agency's

169
00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:20.941
own settings, drawn from NIST's DNS
guide and Infoblox's documentation.

170
00:12:20.941 --> 00:12:25.470
Infoblox does have two current DISA
STIGs, which neither BlueCat nor

171
00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:30.477
EfficientIP has; they're High only,
Defense material, but useful cross-checks.

172
00:12:30.477 --> 00:12:34.640
FIPS 140 is the weak spot,
so let's say it plainly.

173
00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:37.328
Infoblox has no active certificate today.

174
00:12:37.328 --> 00:12:44.056
Its FIPS 140-2 certificates moved to the
historical list with the September 21,

175
00:12:44.056 --> 00:12:49.853
2026 sunset, and four FIPS 140-3
submissions are still in NIST's queue.

176
00:12:49.853 --> 00:12:55.714
There's no current NIAP certificate
either, with NIOS 9.0 in evaluation.

177
00:12:55.714 --> 00:13:00.397
And a note on NIST's DNS deployment guide:
it's the best practical guide we have,

178
00:13:00.397 --> 00:13:03.593
but two of its three authors
are Infoblox employees,

179
00:13:03.593 --> 00:13:08.270
so disclose that whenever you cite
it in support of choosing Infoblox.

180
00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.482
Here's a fair comparison.

181
00:13:13.482 --> 00:13:18.002
On FedRAMP, Infoblox is the only
dedicated DDI vendor with a listing,

182
00:13:18.002 --> 00:13:22.845
though the hyperscalers' government
clouds sit at High, a higher ceiling.

183
00:13:22.845 --> 00:13:31.095
On FIPS 140, BlueCat leads with an active
FIPS 140-3 certificate valid to 2029.

184
00:13:31.095 --> 00:13:34.371
Infoblox is the only
one with its own STIGs,

185
00:13:34.371 --> 00:13:38.957
a High only reference, and has
the broadest DNS security set.

186
00:13:38.957 --> 00:13:41.445
On automation, it has the deepest.

187
00:13:41.445 --> 00:13:47.133
DHCPv6 failover is missing everywhere
we checked, so it's no differentiator.

188
00:13:47.133 --> 00:13:49.375
And alternatives do win in places:

189
00:13:49.375 --> 00:13:54.019
BlueCat's Micetro manages existing
Windows and BIND servers in place,

190
00:13:54.019 --> 00:13:58.183
and a single-cloud workload may
need no third-party plane at all.

191
00:13:58.183 --> 00:14:02.334
So the fair claim is a strong
default for a hybrid federal estate,

192
00:14:02.334 --> 00:14:04.057
not the only viable choice.

193
00:14:04.057 --> 00:14:05.570
Now the risks.

194
00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:13.070
Now the risks, stated plainly,
with how to manage each.

195
00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:18.145
First, cryptographic validation:
no active FIPS certificate today.

196
00:14:18.145 --> 00:14:21.572
If your system needs validated
cryptography at award,

197
00:14:21.572 --> 00:14:26.142
tie go-live to the issued certificate,
or carry the gap as a dated POA&M item,

198
00:14:26.142 --> 00:14:29.570
and ask which NIOS versions
the pending modules cover.

199
00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:32.074
Second, no current NIAP certificate,

200
00:14:32.074 --> 00:14:36.833
and the CDM product list is closed,
so don't promise anyone that status.

201
00:14:36.833 --> 00:14:42.551
Third, vulnerabilities: NIOS has had CVEs
rated as high as 9.8, though none is

202
00:14:42.551 --> 00:14:48.644
in CISA's known exploited catalog, so
write the patch cadence into the ATO plan.

203
00:14:48.644 --> 00:14:53.358
Fourth, NIOS runs on BIND, so ask
for a BIND advisory commitment.

204
00:14:53.358 --> 00:14:57.520
And fifth, the SaaS tops out
at Moderate with zero reuse.

205
00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:05.432
The second set of risks is about
cost, capacity and features.

206
00:15:05.432 --> 00:15:11.184
Universal DDI licensing uses subscription
tokens that expire at the end of the term,

207
00:15:11.184 --> 00:15:16.195
so model total cost over three to five
years, including a hardware refresh.

208
00:15:16.195 --> 00:15:19.108
Size the reporting tier
for the retention you need,

209
00:15:19.108 --> 00:15:21.120
or make the SIEM the system of record.

210
00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:26.137
Plan the hardware refresh around encrypted
DNS, put a stronger signing algorithm

211
00:15:26.137 --> 00:15:31.007
in the build standard, and keep Grid Manager
runbooks for what the Portal can't do.

212
00:15:31.007 --> 00:15:34.983
Ignore stale collateral and cite
only the Marketplace record.

213
00:15:34.983 --> 00:15:38.129
Then turn the risks into
contract obligations:

214
00:15:38.129 --> 00:15:43.211
a certificate timeline with a remedy if
it slips, a BIND advisory commitment,

215
00:15:43.211 --> 00:15:47.430
the FedRAMP boundary diagram,
and a data export guarantee.

216
00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:55.943
Part 2 is about divisions under
central governance, starting with NIOS.

217
00:15:55.943 --> 00:16:00.527
Most agencies aren't one network, and a
central plane only works if divisions can

218
00:16:00.527 --> 00:16:05.400
still do their jobs through it, because a
division that can't will work around it.

219
00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:13.226
The NIOS permission model starts
from the right place: everything is

220
00:16:13.226 --> 00:16:17.588
denied by default, so a new group sees
nothing until you grant it something.

221
00:16:17.588 --> 00:16:22.356
Permissions can cover every object of a
type, or one specific object and what's

222
00:16:22.356 --> 00:16:27.125
inside it, and they inherit downward,
with more specific settings overriding.

223
00:16:27.125 --> 00:16:30.388
When permissions overlap,
the first match wins.

224
00:16:30.388 --> 00:16:34.375
So if one role grants read-only
and a later role grants read-write

225
00:16:34.375 --> 00:16:38.513
on the same records, the admin gets
read-only, which can surprise you.

226
00:16:38.513 --> 00:16:42.290
Be careful with the built-in
DNS and DHCP admin roles,

227
00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:45.013
which are far too broad for a division.

228
00:16:45.013 --> 00:16:49.509
And the governance lever: only
superusers create groups and roles,

229
00:16:49.509 --> 00:16:52.125
so a division can't widen its own rights.

230
00:16:52.125 --> 00:16:54.020
Next, the recipe.

231
00:16:57.770 --> 00:17:01.370
Here's the recipe for scoping
one admin group to one division.

232
00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:05.557
Create a limited-access group with
only the interfaces it needs;

233
00:17:05.557 --> 00:17:09.507
help-desk staff can be restricted
to running approved tasks.

234
00:17:09.507 --> 00:17:13.788
Grant read-write on the division's
own address space and its own zones,

235
00:17:13.788 --> 00:17:18.745
with read-only on the parent view, and
confine its service work to its own servers.

236
00:17:18.745 --> 00:17:20.995
Leave everything else at the default deny.

237
00:17:20.995 --> 00:17:24.519
Then the step people miss: turn
on the Grid setting that makes

238
00:17:24.519 --> 00:17:28.395
address records need rights on both
the zone and the address block.

239
00:17:28.395 --> 00:17:31.324
Without it, a division with
write access to a shared

240
00:17:31.324 --> 00:17:34.533
zone could point a name at
another division's addresses.

241
00:17:34.533 --> 00:17:37.407
That one setting is what
keeps divisions honest.

242
00:17:37.407 --> 00:17:40.360
Next, the constructs that separate them.

243
00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:46.927
Three constructs separate
divisions on NIOS,

244
00:17:46.927 --> 00:17:49.448
and each has a rule
that shapes the design.

245
00:17:49.448 --> 00:17:53.327
Network views let two divisions
hold the same addresses,

246
00:17:53.327 --> 00:17:58.444
but a DHCP server serves only one view,
so a division with overlapping space

247
00:17:58.444 --> 00:18:03.973
needs its own DHCP servers, and nothing
on NIOS enforces uniqueness across views.

248
00:18:03.973 --> 00:18:08.895
DNS views give different answers to
different clients, and the first match wins,

249
00:18:08.895 --> 00:18:12.848
so a catch-all external view placed
first hides the internal one.

250
00:18:12.848 --> 00:18:17.648
And extensible attributes tag objects
with things like Division or cost center.

251
00:18:17.648 --> 00:18:21.626
Two cautions: a required tag
can be skipped through the API,

252
00:18:21.626 --> 00:18:26.434
so automation must enforce it, and
tags drive reports, not permissions.

253
00:18:26.434 --> 00:18:29.035
Access is always scoped by objects.

254
00:18:29.035 --> 00:18:30.780
Next, approvals.

255
00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:37.023
The approval workflow
is how central keeps a

256
00:18:37.023 --> 00:18:39.718
hand on division changes
without doing the work.

257
00:18:39.718 --> 00:18:44.465
A division submits, a central group
approves, reschedules or rejects, and

258
00:18:44.465 --> 00:18:49.767
approved tasks run with the submitter's
own permissions and land in the audit log.

259
00:18:49.767 --> 00:18:54.983
The riskiest operations, like signing
zones, rolling keys, or resizing networks,

260
00:18:54.983 --> 00:18:59.567
are blocked for submitters entirely,
which suits a federal model well.

261
00:18:59.567 --> 00:19:00.555
Now the gap.

262
00:19:00.555 --> 00:19:05.092
Infoblox's own page says CSV
imports do not require approvals.

263
00:19:05.092 --> 00:19:07.626
Leave that permission
in place and a division

264
00:19:07.626 --> 00:19:10.293
can load a thousand records
with no review at all.

265
00:19:10.293 --> 00:19:13.239
So remove CSV import from
every division group,

266
00:19:13.239 --> 00:19:16.942
and give bulk loads to a central
group under a change ticket.

267
00:19:16.942 --> 00:19:18.680
Next, sign-in.

268
00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:25.062
Who can sign in, and
which group they land in,

269
00:19:25.062 --> 00:19:27.393
is the other half of division scoping.

270
00:19:27.393 --> 00:19:32.510
NIOS maps directory groups to its own
admin groups, with the first match winning,

271
00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:37.006
so an AD group per division can decide
where an administrator belongs.

272
00:19:37.006 --> 00:19:40.924
For federal staff, the
important path is certificate

273
00:19:40.924 --> 00:19:44.130
sign-in with CAC or PIV,
checked against OCSP.

274
00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:46.708
SAML works too, including with Entra,

275
00:19:46.708 --> 00:19:50.867
where the user lands in the
group whose name exactly matches.

276
00:19:50.867 --> 00:19:56.101
A few things to test: certificate sign-in
to the API can conflict with certain cloud

277
00:19:56.101 --> 00:19:59.025
licenses, SAML-only users
get no command line,

278
00:19:59.025 --> 00:20:03.180
and it's not confirmed whether Entra
sends the group name or its ID.

279
00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:07.270
Order mappings carefully, and
keep a local break-glass admin.

280
00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:13.683
Three topics close the NIOS module.

281
00:20:13.683 --> 00:20:19.098
First, a division still running Windows
DNS and DHCP can be managed from the Grid,

282
00:20:19.098 --> 00:20:23.282
with the division's group given
rights on its own servers only,

283
00:20:23.282 --> 00:20:25.744
not the system-wide Microsoft roles.

284
00:20:25.744 --> 00:20:29.754
Second, what stays central in
every design: the Grid Master,

285
00:20:29.754 --> 00:20:35.100
DNSSEC keys and signing, security policy,
the definitions of groups and roles,

286
00:20:35.100 --> 00:20:38.358
sign-in policy, and the
top-level address blocks.

287
00:20:38.358 --> 00:20:41.870
Third, a limitation that
surprises almost everyone.

288
00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:45.220
NIOS reporting doesn't
filter by permissions,

289
00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:50.333
so a division with the reporting app
sees agency-wide DNS and DHCP data.

290
00:20:50.333 --> 00:20:53.357
That matters a great deal
for a law-enforcement bureau.

291
00:20:53.357 --> 00:20:57.033
So scope reporting by sharing
dashboards per division instead.

292
00:20:57.033 --> 00:20:59.460
Next, splitting by service.

293
00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:06.072
Divisions aren't the only way to cut DDI.

294
00:21:06.072 --> 00:21:11.192
Many agencies split it by service,
with DNS, DHCP and IPAM teams working

295
00:21:11.192 --> 00:21:15.885
across the whole agency, and NIOS
supports that with the same model.

296
00:21:15.885 --> 00:21:20.023
This is an illustrative design,
not a validated configuration.

297
00:21:20.023 --> 00:21:26.161
The DNS team gets its views, zones and
DNS servers, with no network rights.

298
00:21:26.161 --> 00:21:31.157
The DHCP team is where the catch is:
a permission on a network also covers

299
00:21:31.157 --> 00:21:35.985
its ranges, so grant the ranges and
fixed addresses directly instead.

300
00:21:35.985 --> 00:21:39.097
The IPAM team gets the address containers,

301
00:21:39.097 --> 00:21:43.135
with an explicit deny on DHCP
ranges if that team owns them.

302
00:21:43.135 --> 00:21:46.384
And the same Grid-wide
setting from before becomes

303
00:21:46.384 --> 00:21:49.710
a deliberate check between
the DNS and IPAM teams.

304
00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:51.800
Next, combining both.

305
00:21:55.550 --> 00:22:00.250
Service teams and divisions aren't
alternatives; most agencies combine them,

306
00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:04.362
because a grant can be scoped by
service and by address space at once.

307
00:22:04.362 --> 00:22:06.325
Again, it's illustrative.

308
00:22:06.325 --> 00:22:10.910
The central DNS team keeps the
agency's top zone and DNSSEC signing,

309
00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:16.578
the central IPAM team keeps the top-level
blocks and hands each division its own,

310
00:22:16.578 --> 00:22:20.163
and divisions take over DHCP
inside their own space.

311
00:22:20.163 --> 00:22:22.600
On Universal DDI the picture changes,

312
00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:27.002
and there's a documentation conflict
over whether separate DNS and

313
00:22:27.002 --> 00:22:31.325
DHCP roles are supported in this
release, so confirm with Infoblox.

314
00:22:31.325 --> 00:22:36.621
And one caution applies however you split:
on NIOS, reports ignore permissions,

315
00:22:36.621 --> 00:22:40.288
so anyone with the reporting
app sees agency-wide data.

316
00:22:40.288 --> 00:22:42.600
Now Universal DDI.

317
00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:51.400
Module 4 covers divisions on Universal
DDI, and the operating model.

318
00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:56.873
We'll look at its access views and IPAM
Federation, then four delegation patterns,

319
00:22:56.873 --> 00:23:00.710
a worked example, who does
what, and a rollout sequence.

320
00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:08.929
On Universal DDI, the division
construct is the access view,

321
00:23:08.929 --> 00:23:12.122
a container for a
division's DDI resources.

322
00:23:12.122 --> 00:23:15.054
Corporate admins create
one level of sub-views,

323
00:23:15.054 --> 00:23:18.586
and divisional admins can't
see anything outside theirs.

324
00:23:18.586 --> 00:23:22.035
Assign an IP space and
everything in it follows.

325
00:23:22.035 --> 00:23:27.507
The constraints are significant: users
and roles are defined only centrally,

326
00:23:27.507 --> 00:23:31.953
access views can't be renamed or
deleted, so name them carefully,

327
00:23:31.953 --> 00:23:35.373
and service users with API
keys can't log in to one.

328
00:23:35.373 --> 00:23:41.087
The big gain over NIOS is IPAM Federation,
which enforces address uniqueness

329
00:23:41.087 --> 00:23:47.235
across Universal DDI, NIOS and cloud IPAM,
including deliberate overlap behind NAT.

330
00:23:47.235 --> 00:23:50.697
And remember the documentation
conflict on supported roles.

331
00:23:50.697 --> 00:23:52.423
Confirm it with Infoblox.

332
00:23:52.423 --> 00:23:54.440
Next, the patterns.

333
00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:03.190
Put together, these give four delegation
patterns, and most agencies mix them.

334
00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:07.458
Pattern A is one central Grid with
scoped admin groups and approvals,

335
00:24:07.458 --> 00:24:11.727
the default, with risks like permission
sprawl and the import bypass.

336
00:24:11.727 --> 00:24:15.407
Pattern B gives a division its
own servers inside the Grid,

337
00:24:15.407 --> 00:24:19.853
for local resilience, while signed
zones still keep a central primary.

338
00:24:19.853 --> 00:24:24.031
Pattern C gives a division its
own network view or IP space,

339
00:24:24.031 --> 00:24:27.427
the only way to hold
overlapping address space.

340
00:24:27.427 --> 00:24:32.763
And pattern D delegates a zone to servers
the division runs itself: full autonomy,

341
00:24:32.763 --> 00:24:34.992
but central loses the audit trail,

342
00:24:34.992 --> 00:24:38.416
security policy coverage
and signing for that zone.

343
00:24:38.416 --> 00:24:42.396
Use A by default, B for
resilience, C only for overlap,

344
00:24:42.396 --> 00:24:45.600
and D only where autonomy is worth it.

345
00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:53.376
Here's an illustrative design,
not a validated configuration,

346
00:24:53.376 --> 00:24:55.587
for an agency with four divisions.

347
00:24:55.587 --> 00:24:58.299
Headquarters is the
central operations team.

348
00:24:58.299 --> 00:25:01.536
A law-enforcement bureau
needs confidentiality,

349
00:25:01.536 --> 00:25:03.825
so it gets its own servers, pattern B.

350
00:25:03.825 --> 00:25:07.312
A regional office still
runs Windows DNS and DHCP,

351
00:25:07.312 --> 00:25:11.225
managed from the Grid with
rights on those servers only.

352
00:25:11.225 --> 00:25:16.647
And a research lab whose range overlaps
headquarters gets its own network view

353
00:25:16.647 --> 00:25:21.987
and DHCP servers, pattern C, with its zone
delegated to its own servers, pattern D.

354
00:25:21.987 --> 00:25:24.993
Across the Grid: object
permissions turned on,

355
00:25:24.993 --> 00:25:28.709
so headquarters can't point a
name into the bureau's space,

356
00:25:28.709 --> 00:25:32.505
no CSV import for any division,
one approval workflow each,

357
00:25:32.505 --> 00:25:36.180
and the bureau's data kept
out of shared dashboards.

358
00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:40.855
Who does what?

359
00:25:40.855 --> 00:25:43.324
This matrix is adapted from Book 08's,

360
00:25:43.324 --> 00:25:48.180
which separates deciding from doing,
with a column added for divisions.

361
00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.851
The CIO decides the platform
and how divisions are scoped,

362
00:25:51.851 --> 00:25:55.676
and the central DDI team builds
it, with divisions consulted,

363
00:25:55.676 --> 00:25:59.118
because a scope they can't
work in becomes a workaround.

364
00:25:59.118 --> 00:26:03.806
The authorizing official decides whether
a cloud management plane is authorized.

365
00:26:03.806 --> 00:26:06.757
The CISO decides the control requirements,

366
00:26:06.757 --> 00:26:10.667
like signing and security policy,
and central DDI runs them.

367
00:26:10.667 --> 00:26:14.253
Standard record changes are
where delegation pays off:

368
00:26:14.253 --> 00:26:17.993
central defines them in advance,
and divisions do the work.

369
00:26:17.993 --> 00:26:20.167
High-impact changes stay central.

370
00:26:20.167 --> 00:26:26.310
And an encrypted DNS exception with no
end date goes to the authorizing official.

371
00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:34.650
To close, the rollout follows Book
08's order, adapted for divisions:

372
00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:38.405
inventory everything first,
build the central pieces,

373
00:26:38.405 --> 00:26:43.495
coexist with the Windows servers in
place, onboard divisions one at a time

374
00:26:43.495 --> 00:26:48.335
starting with pattern A, federate
the clouds, then harden and evidence.

375
00:26:48.335 --> 00:26:52.487
The decisions to take now are the
platform model for each impact level,

376
00:26:52.487 --> 00:26:56.352
the delegation pattern per division,
whether to wait for the FIPS

377
00:26:56.352 --> 00:26:59.860
certificate or proceed with a
POA&M, and where logs will live.

378
00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:04.498
Then send Infoblox the procurement
asks and lab-test the open questions.

379
00:27:04.498 --> 00:27:08.823
In the program's analysis, Infoblox
is one of the strongest ways to

380
00:27:08.823 --> 00:27:13.309
build a governed DDI plane, as long
as you manage its risks in the open.

381
00:27:13.309 --> 00:27:18.235
This is research under review, not an
endorsement by any federal agency.

382
00:27:18.235 --> 00:27:19.070
Thank you.
